A||||18||turbo time||Weight Distribution||||1||
Z||000000||turbo time||12-24-2001||02:50 PM||||85 Fiero<BR>I've got the spare tire, A/C out already, battery is moved to the front. <P>What are some other common things to pull when removing weight (still keeping it pleasant to drive though)?<P>radio stays, racing seats are in the plans for next summer.<P>I don't plan to remove anything that would comprimise the safety of the vehicle since I'll be driving around with my wife in the car most of the summer.||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||12-24-2001||08:53 PM||||window glass is heavy <BR>under carrpit insulation<BR>insulation in eng compartment<BR>power anything windows mirrors doorlocks<BR>lighter wheels $$<BR>alloy block and heads but $$$<BR>body parts hoods and  r deck and springs and hinges  f/g replacements pinned in place<BR>trim inside and out<BR>wing<BR>trunk inside guts<BR>unnessary wires like a/c controls<BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||12-25-2001||02:19 AM||||Start to consider carbon hood/decklids...<P>With the battery up front, your weight distirbution is frankly already pretty darned good for a mid-engine car (you do NOT want 50/50 in mid-engine!).<P>The next step would be to change to an alloy engine, like a quad4 or Al block v6.  $$$$$ again.<P>I would look into upgrading your suspension with coilovers (front and rear) etc next...<P><BR>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||theogre||12-25-2001||01:59 PM||||Power window motors don't weigh very much. 2#, maybe 3# each... In a street driven car you'd likely hate youself if you remove them. I couln't stand not being able to open/close the passenger's window while drivning so I added the power windows.<P>If you already have aluminum wheels, you might loose a pound or two each by changing to differant wheels/tires. If you by some odd chance have steel wheels you can loose a bunch.<P>You'll need to know what your current wheel configuration weight is then you'll need both the rim and tire weight of the new stuff. Allot of that info is on the tire/rim makers web sites. A aramid (Kevlar) belted tire, if you can find them, might be slightly lighter than equivalent steel belted. <P>Changing rotating mass small amounts can have a bigger effect than even large changes to the body. Even then in most cases you're talking hundredths to 10ths of a second most of the time.<P>Also keep in mind that what you gain thru reducing tire weight may be negated by using a wider tire. Tire combinations are one of those more art than science things allot of the time.<P>The sound deadener under the carpet and so on can be quite heavy but the insulation in the engine bay doesn't weigh much. Pulling it out on the sides of the engine bay will make working on the car more pleasant. Leave the stuff on the firewall there.<P>You want the car streetable.... There aren't a huge number of things you can do without major alterations. And even then, if they aren't done very carfully they could weaken the car, likely reducing safety and increasing body flex. I wouldn't recomend any frame alterations.<P>You can save considerable weight during racing by not filling the fuel tank all the way. Just Don't go so low that the fuel pump goes dry in corners etc. What's a gallong of fuel weigh? 6-7#? I can't remember. If you drop to 1/2 tank then you drop around 5 gallons worth of mass. That's a pretty good chunk.<P>If you made a fiberglass version of the center console, the part with the glove box, you could loose a few pounds. That thing is fairly hefty.<P>The dash didn't seem to weigh very much when I had it out last year... There's not much under it that could go either. I think the rear part of the console weighs almost as much as the dash.<P>Depending on what type race, you'll also want to play with tire pressure. Tire pressure can be used to tune both rolling resistance and spring rate. (Don't inflate tires above the max rating in the sidewall!) You'll want a good dial or digital gauge for that. Just a 1/2 PSI change can make a differance.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroPerformance||12-26-2001||01:11 AM||||Are you trying to evenly distribute or cut weight? If you are trying to distribute why did you remove weight from the front and then put weight up there by moving the battery?||||reg||1
Z||000005||ZeroFiero||12-26-2001||12:32 PM||||Hmmm.... Why don't I want 50/50 distribution<BR>in a mid-engine? If it is fine for a front<BR>engine, what difference does it make in the<BR>MERD? Moment of inertia concerns or what?<P>Thanks,<BR>ZeroFiero||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick's Dad||12-26-2001||12:47 PM||||How about filling the tires with Helium? That's what bicyclists do.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||turbo time||12-27-2001||08:36 PM||||The battery is going to the front because I need room for the Talon engine, tranny, intercooler pipes. The Talon engine runs quite hot with the turbo and the more room that you have in the engine bay the better.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||12-27-2001||11:17 PM||||In a mid-engine car, you generally need some 'heft' in the rear to control and plant things in turns.  Conversely, if the front end weighs too much, you develop plough (understeer)  Yes, it has to do with the MOI provided by that big honking engine. (which are different in a front vs mid-engine car, even if you got the actual tire weights to both be 50/50!)<P>You really want about 45/55 (not much, but enough to 'tick over' the car into a 'rear heavy' type of handling.  Plant that rear end.  <P>Note that these are generalizations made on traditional chassis design for mid-engine road cars.   Not nessicarily applicable to raceing cars.<P><BR>PS: Helium will bleed out *through* the rubber fairly fast (it's molecularily *very* small) Pure Nitrogen on the other hand, has better temp charestericts than plain air, and will stay in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ZeroFiero:<BR><B>Hmmm.... Why don't I want 50/50 distribution<BR>in a mid-engine? If it is fine for a front<BR>engine, what difference does it make in the<BR>MERD? Moment of inertia concerns or what?<P>Thanks,<BR>ZeroFiero</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000009||ZeroFiero||12-28-2001||03:49 PM||||Thanks, Artherd  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>By "rear-heavy" you mean oversteer? <BR>or with 45/55 is it neutral?<BR>No big questions, and thanks for the<BR>info<P>ZeroFiero||||reg||3
Z||000010||artherd||12-29-2001||12:25 AM||||Yep, a little 'rear heaviness' will actually neutral-out the car.<P>However *too much* rear bias will of course kick the rear end waaay out (oversteer!)  (not to mention do a bunch of other ugly things, like lift the front wheels under power in turns (can be a big problem in high powered supercars)<P>The trick is to find a balance, and 45/55 or so is usually about it.  Just enough to 'set' the car into a certin way of handling (neutural) so that over or understeer can be selected by how you abuse the gas/brakes, and the natural line is a perfect 4-wheel drift!<P>Of course, everything is a tradeoff, and you HAVE to throw quite a few of the many the relative Mass Centres of the whole car into play in 3-space before you can acurately even begin to model how it will perform...  <P>Mid-engine chassis dynamics are really complex  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   (but so darned FUN!)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ZeroFiero:<BR><B>Thanks, Artherd   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>By "rear-heavy" you mean oversteer? <BR>or with 45/55 is it neutral?<BR>No big questions, and thanks for the<BR>info<P>ZeroFiero</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||California Kid||12-29-2001||07:22 AM||||My car was checked at all four wheels after the V8 conversion in '93. The numbers resulted in 45% Front and 55% Rear. This is with a 4 Core Radiator up front, alum head V8, and Battery moved to left side of engine compartment. This is also a sunroof model with AC. Handling is incredible!!!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||The Fieromaster||12-29-2001||10:56 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by turbo time:<BR><B>The battery is going to the front because I need room for the Talon engine, tranny, intercooler pipes. The Talon engine runs quite hot with the turbo and the more room that you have in the engine bay the better.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I dont know how your running your pipes but i am running the IC pipes into the passenger fender and i am going to invest in a custom scoop and run the intercooler right behind it... then makeing sure that the air can vent out... Possibly into wheel well as it does in the dsm's!  Also you wont have such a huge heat problem if you use header wrap on everything... possibly including the exhaust side of the turbo!  I am using the DSM tranny so im not sure if i am going to need to modify anything else (axles will need to be however)... The dsm tranny is pretty heft compaird to the Fiero tranny wich is totaly prone to blow with monster power... Im not lying either... I have blown 3 Fiero trannies!  If you need any sugestions let me know... Im working on some wiring diagrams now to integrate them (the engine harnesses) together!  However i am NOT using a Fiero engine harness at all..  I am running the eclipse gauges to make things easier and with the revere indeglo gauges it will look good too!  Also using the eclipse computer to make engine control easier.  This all should make splicing easy.... Well it is making it easy as i have found.  Well let me know if you need help...<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">          <FONT COLOR="red"><B>"Raced That, Wrecked Better"</FONT></B>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">     <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/thefieromastersig.gif">       <BR><B>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000</B><BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>AOL IM: Fieromaster</B></FONT><BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">1985 GT ~ Purple, Fastback, Black and Yellow Interior, Planning 4G63 Turbo DSM swap for it!</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="maroon">1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX ~ Maroon w/ Hella Balls!(GSX = AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="green">1992 Mitsubishi Eclipse GS ~ soon will be turboed</FONT><BR></B><p>[This message has been edited by The Fieromaster (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||turbo time||12-29-2001||11:27 AM||||I'm probably going with Haltech. I have the TMO stage 3 ecu for the dsm engine but I hate the MAS system. I'll also be removing the trunk area for the intercooler with a couple draft fans, I don't want the lag assosiated with such long piping to the front of the car. A custom scoop under the car should provide enough cool air for an efficient intercooler.<P><BR>I'll worry about gauges when I get there, I want to get the thing going first.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Philphine||12-29-2001||02:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>My car was checked at all four wheels after the V8 conversion in '93. The numbers resulted in 45% Front and 55% Rear. This is with a 4 Core Radiator up front, alum head V8, and Battery moved to left side of engine compartment. This is also a sunroof model with AC. Handling is incredible!!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>that's encouraging, but also dosn't match what's normaly heard with sbc swaps (or it could be a rumor that keeps getting repeated by those not really in the know. kinda like i'm doing now. oops) i guess some consideraton has to be made for wheel/tire changes (didn't you say you're at 255 width rear tires now? i know rear engine porsches always have a lot of rear tire) and suspension changes. guess i'll need to go back and see what gets mentioned wheel/tire/suspension wise in the old v8 swap threads. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||12-29-2001||03:59 PM||||Aluminum heads help more than one would think!  Total weight gain is now in the ~100lb range.   This is a passenger.<P>I'm still going with a Northstar because it's lighter (and revs to 7grand stock, 9grand with mods!)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B> that's encouraging, but also dosn't match what's normaly heard with sbc swaps (or it could be a rumor that keeps getting repeated by those not really in the know. kinda like i'm doing now. oops) i guess some consideraton has to be made for wheel/tire changes (didn't you say you're at 255 width rear tires now? i know rear engine porsches always have a lot of rear tire) and suspension changes. guess i'll need to go back and see what gets mentioned wheel/tire/suspension wise in the old v8 swap threads. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000016||The Fieromaster||12-30-2001||12:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by turbo time:<BR><B>I'm probably going with Haltech. I have the TMO stage 3 ecu for the dsm engine but I hate the MAS system. I'll also be removing the trunk area for the intercooler with a couple draft fans, I don't want the lag assosiated with such long piping to the front of the car. A custom scoop under the car should provide enough cool air for an efficient intercooler.<P><BR>I'll worry about gauges when I get there, I want to get the thing going first.....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I think running an intercooler to the front is retarded!!!  Lol so we agree on that... what i meant is a scoop behind the passenger door and place the intercooler behind scoop with a pull fan behind it... All right in the rear passenger wheel well.  I dont think i made that clear before... coulda been easily read wrong<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||California Kid||12-30-2001||12:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B> that's encouraging, but also dosn't match what's normaly heard with sbc swaps (or it could be a rumor that keeps getting repeated by those not really in the know. kinda like i'm doing now. oops) i guess some consideraton has to be made for wheel/tire changes (didn't you say you're at 255 width rear tires now? i know rear engine porsches always have a lot of rear tire) and suspension changes. guess i'll need to go back and see what gets mentioned wheel/tire/suspension wise in the old v8 swap threads. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Vehicle weight change after the swap was plus 90 lbs, keep in mind that's with a 4 core radiator up front filled up. Vette engine ('91 L98) with the only thing being iron is the block, Sanderson tube headers (not cast iron). The weight difference included 16 inch prime alum wheels with 225x50 fronts, 245x50 rear Nitto's. Have since changed to BFG Comp T/A VR4's, fronts are now 215x50 and rears 245x50 with 16 inch OZ wheels. Koni's all around with 1.5 shortened stock '88 springs, everything urethane.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||ray b||12-30-2001||12:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ZeroFiero:<BR><B>Thanks, Artherd   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>By "rear-heavy" you mean oversteer? <BR>or with 45/55 is it neutral?<BR>No big questions, and thanks for the<BR>info<P>ZeroFiero</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>SPRINGS SHOCKS AND ROLL BARS can all change over/under steer and NEUTRAL is allso about speed, rate of curve, tyres and tyre temps and type of pavements grips PLUS YOU STYLE and what you expect/want.<BR>a good way to learn free is GRAND PRIX LEGENDS free demo SIM down load it at<BR> <A HREF="http://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.download.index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.download.index.html</A> <P>you can play with springs and shocks rollbars toein/out caster and camber<BR>without cost or risk the 67 cars pre=wings with hard tyres are close to a fiero or enuff to play and learn.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
